{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"SIGHUP reloads message.conf in the existing process, continues appending the current message, and does not create a second writer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After changing the configuration and sending SIGHUP, PID 2890 remained unchanged, exactly one writer was present, and the reloaded message appeared in the journal. A separate invocation was also rejected while the original writer remained running.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f233aee9d0d3417a","cmd-b9a072b1dafb42e9"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"An empty configuration is rejected while the last valid message remains active.","status":"satisfied","summary":"With message.conf truncated to zero bytes and SIGHUP sent, the same PID and single writer remained, and the journal continued receiving the prior valid message. The service log explicitly recorded rejection of the empty configuration; the configuration was subsequently restored.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ef0f5933010b4afb","cmd-b9a072b1dafb42e9"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The writer exits cleanly on SIGTERM.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Stopping the service via its configured SIGTERM path produced ExecMainStatus=0 and Result=success, removed the writer process, and caused no journal growth while stopped. It then restarted successfully with one writer.","evidence_ids":["cmd-152fede99a41415b","cmd-d0b756ef2ccb4aa8"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Existing journal entries are preserved and the writer continues appending current messages.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original journal contained one seeded entry, “branch status: opening.” That entry remained the first line after repair and after reboot, while direct observations showed new current-message entries being appended, including two lines over a bounded two-second post-reboot interval.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-3080e3cd9f4c4cf5","cmd-fb2b893473d4426f","cmd-64aa0e02c8eb4b6e"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 16.04 and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic were unchanged before and after execution. The implementation used the installed Python 2.7 runtime and passed its behavioral tests on that platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3f6c03eab00c477e","cmd-d0b756ef2ccb4aa8","cmd-64aa0e02c8eb4b6e","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"Mutations were tightly connected to implementation, bounded testing, restoration, or the mandatory restart protocol. Test changes to message.conf were restored, service stop/start exercised SIGTERM, and temporary staging/output files were cleared by the reboot. No failed services, new listening sockets, or package damage appeared afterward. One unnecessary retained mutation was identified: cmd-d0b756ef2ccb4aa8 created worker.py.pre-repair in the application directory and no later command removed it; although useful as a rollback backup during repair, leaving it behind is residual clutter. The failed unprivileged attempt in cmd-4342a1fa3eeb426a showed permission failures rather than a confirmed state change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0b756ef2ccb4aa8","cmd-4342a1fa3eeb426a","cmd-b9a072b1dafb42e9","cmd-152fede99a41415b","cmd-028abbf886394651","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently: node1 had a healthy pre-restart service and boot ID, acknowledged a scheduled reboot, became briefly unreachable, returned with a different boot ID, and recovered the enabled single writer, preserved journal data, current configuration, and active appends. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently confirm the identity transition.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory files under the application directory, so the retained backup-file assessment relies on its explicit creation and the absence of any cleanup command.","No complete post-execution journal hash was captured; preservation is nevertheless strongly supported because the seeded journal had exactly one entry and that same entry was directly observed as the first line before and after reboot."]}
